On Sat, Sep 16, 2017 at 11:11 PM, <mrw...@mail.harvard.edu> wrote:
> Hi,
>
> I'm really enjoying the capabilities of this great package. I'm trying to fit a 4-factor IRT model with about ~100 items and response data from ~16,000 individuals. This is a computationally intensive model on one-core, even for the MH-RM estimator, so I'm hoping to take advantage of my 8-core PC and the mirtCluster() feature. Unfortunately, I don't appear to be successful in fitting the model in parallel--the CPU usage from my MS Window's Task Manager suggests that only one core is being utilized. Below I include replicable code for a simulation of the situation that I am facing.

The issue isn't with your code, it's that the EM algorithm is mainly done on a single core. Only certain embarrassingly-parallel subroutines are included via mirtCluster(), such as things like computing the S-EM estimate of the ACOV, Monte Carlo log-likelihoods, etc. The EM unfortunately isn't embarissingly paralell (the E-step can be approximately parallelized with OpenMP, upto to storing all the components into the complete-data table, though my previous attempts at doing so have been less successful).

The only thing I might recommend is that with a 4 factor model you probably want to use quasi-Monte Carlo integration rather than the default EM (method = 'QMCEM') because otherwise the integration grid may be too sparse, and perhaps take a sub-sample of your large dataset (e.g., 5000-8000) and only estimate the parameters of that subset because the estimates shouldn't change much from the full sample given N of that magnitude. HTH.

Phil

